# gh-checkout.dl — ghcacher's OTHER half, as a datalog program.
#
# ghcacher (~/projects/ghcacher) does two things. gh-cache.dl is the first:
# poll the GitHub API into SQLite. This is the second: keep local git CHECKOUTS
# current on disk (ghcacher's checkout.rs). For every configured repo it clones
# what is missing, fetches origin, and NON-DESTRUCTIVELY keeps the named branch
# current. A `dl --lsp` daemon on an interval IS the watch loop.
#
# `checkout(repo, branch, pr_heads)` is the built-in demand sink that runs the
# sweep. Per row:
#   * repo     = a config slug (or "." for self / a path). A config repo whose
#                root is absent but has a `url` is cloned first.
#   * branch   = the branch to keep current. Empty = discover origin/HEAD.
#   * pr_heads = "1"/"true" also mirrors +refs/pull/*/head into refs/remotes/pr/*
#                (the ghcacher fetch_pr_branches option); "0" skips it.
# On the default branch + clean working tree it fast-forwards via
# `merge --ff-only origin/<branch>`. On the default branch + dirty, it SKIPs
# (the operator's work — never stashed, never overwritten). On the default
# branch + diverged, it SKIPs (no force-update, no reset). On any OTHER branch
# (or detached) it moves only the ref pointer with `git branch -f` and leaves
# HEAD + the working tree exactly where they are. Repos sweep in parallel on a
# narrow pool; a failure skips loudly. `DL_NO_FETCH=1` skips the network.
#
# A bare `dl gh-checkout.dl` is a READ: the sink does NOT fire from a `?`
# query (a query must never trigger a destructive network sweep). To fire it
# one-shot, opt in with `--apply` (or `DL_APPLY_SINKS=1`). To PREVIEW what it
# would do without mutating anything, set `DL_CHECKOUT_DRY_RUN=1` (rows land
# in `checkout_plan`, not `checkout_done`). The daemon's poll loop and
# `--watch`/`--settle` always drain on their cadence.
#
# Run (daemon):  dl --lsp examples/gh-checkout.dl
# One-shot:      dl examples/gh-checkout.dl --apply

# --- read our own config ----------------------------------------------------
# The repo set is the built-in `repo` relation, populated from the `[[repos]]`
# config (the same config gh-cache.dl and every multi-repo program reads). Keep
# every configured repo current on its default branch: empty `branch` lets the
# sink follow each repo's origin/HEAD, so a repo tracking `master` and one
# tracking `main` both work from one rule.
#
# Do NOT gate on `url`: the `repo` rel carries a `url` only for repos configured
# WITH a clone url, and an already-cloned staging repo (slug + root, no url) has
# an EMPTY url — gating `url != ""` would silently sweep nothing in exactly the
# ghcacher deployment this is for. Every row of `repo` is a configured repo.
#
# WARNING: when NO config is loaded, `repo` is just the self `--root` repo, so
# this rule would target YOUR dev checkout (a `merge --ff-only` against origin on
# your current branch). Run it against a config of STAGING clones, not bare in a
# working repo.
checkout(slug, "", "0") <- repo(slug, root, url).

# Read the sweep OUTCOME back (one row per repo): action is
# ff/branch-f/skip, ok is 1/0, detail is the git result. Queryable from
# a live daemon (stderr goes to daemon.log; this rel is how you confirm it
# fired). `DL_CHECKOUT_DRY_RUN=1` writes the same shape to `checkout_plan`
# instead, previewing each action without running `merge --ff-only` or `branch -f`.
? checkout_done(repo, branch, action, ok, detail).
